Uploading a commit's objects concurrently instead of one round trip at a time.

host:     AWS c6a.8xlarge, 32 vCPU, us-east-1, temporary in-region S3 bucket
build:    release; "serial" rows are from raw/metal-delta-pack-s3.log and
          raw/metal-golden-root-s3.log, same instance type and same setup
date_utc: 2026-07-27

Before this change, `commit_pack` uploaded its data objects in a plain `for` loop
and `image::publish` did the same, so a commit cost one blocking HTTPS round trip
per object (~33 ms measured). Both now upload with a bounded pool
(MAX_UPLOAD_CONCURRENCY = 16); the join still happens before any map node
references the objects, so AFTER_DATA_UPLOAD means what it always meant.

--- FLUSH: how long a Guest's fsync stalls -------------------------------------

48 chunks touched, one 64 KiB page each (delta packs -- publishes 3 MiB):
  serial       1669.3 ms
  concurrent    243.5 ms      6.9x faster

48 chunks fully rewritten (full 4 MiB bases -- publishes 192 MiB):
  serial       4913.5 ms
  concurrent   1429.9 ms      3.4x faster   (= 134 MB/s, still not the pipe)

sparse row by size, concurrent:
dirty chunks  dirty pages  published MiB     flush ms   ms per pub MiB   sha256 MiB/s
           1            1           0.06        109.6          1753.58           1678
           8            8           0.50        147.5           295.04           1687
          16           16           1.00        233.0           232.96           1302
          32           32           2.00        217.8           108.89           1455
          48           48           3.00        243.5            81.17           1566

dense row by size, concurrent:
dirty chunks  published MiB     flush ms   ms per pub MiB
           8          32.00        291.2             9.10
          32         128.00        797.2             6.23
          48         192.00       1429.9             7.45

**This restores delta packs.** Delta pack versus full-chunk publishing, 48 chunks:

  serial uploads:     4913.5 -> 1669.3 ms   2.9x
  concurrent uploads: 1429.9 ->  243.5 ms   5.9x

The earlier finding that "delta packs are worth less on S3" was really "serial
uploads were hiding them": when every object costs a round trip and the round
trips do not overlap, cutting bytes 64x cannot show through. Overlap the round
trips and the byte reduction is visible again. Flush latency no longer flattens
with size either -- 8 chunks 147 ms, 48 chunks 243 ms, where serially it was
374 ms -> 1669 ms.

--- Golden root: publish and provision -----------------------------------------

Publishing a base image once (this is the cost the first launch on a node pays):
  image MiB     serial   concurrent
         64    1601.8      308.9      5.2x
        256    6719.0      957.1      7.0x
        512    8310.1     1155.7      7.2x

512 MiB drops from 8.3 s to 1.2 s, which mostly removes the reason to pre-publish
images at node start.

Provisioning one root disk (the create path), 512 MiB image:
  copy       15472.6 ->  5334.7 ms   (the baseline got 2.9x faster too)
  reference     89.7 ->    86.0 ms   (unchanged: it never uploaded anything)
  golden root advantage: 172x -> 62x

Ten concurrent creates, 256 MiB image:
  copy       27230.0 -> 22488.4 ms   (only 1.2x: ten threads already kept the
                                      pipe busy, so per-commit concurrency adds
                                      little on top)
  reference    167.4 ->   157.4 ms
  golden root advantage: 162x -> 143x

Reported honestly: concurrency speeds up the *old* path as well, so golden root's
multiple over it shrinks. It is still 62x on a single create, and the absolute
figure is what matters -- 86 ms against 5.3 s.

--- A new finding: cold reads are still serial ---------------------------------

Reading 64 MiB of a cold volume back from S3:
        path       cold read ms              MiB/s
        copy              881.9                 73
   reference              818.1                 78

73-78 MiB/s from a 12.5 Gbps instance. Hydration fetches one chunk per GET and
does not overlap them, which is the same shape as the upload problem and has not
been fixed. It also means the cold-read amplification of delta chains (up to 5
objects per chunk) currently multiplies a serial cost. Next.
